{"name":"Sconce","hex":"#ae935d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sconce","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fixture for a light, which holds it and provides a screen against wind or against a naked flame or lightbulb.\n\nA candlestick (holder for a candle, especially a circular tube, with a brim, into which a candle is inserted), either with a handle for carrying, or with a bracket for attaching to a wall.","A candlestick (holder for a candle, especially a circular tube, with a brim, into which a candle is inserted), either with a handle for carrying, or with a bracket for attaching to a wall."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A head or a skull.","A poll tax; a mulct or fine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sconce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sconce (fortification)","blocks":[{"text":"A sconce or schanze is a protective fortification, such as an earthwork, often placed on a mound as a defensive work for artillery, It was used primarily in Northern Europe from the late Middle Ages until the 19th century. This type of fortification was common during the English Civil War, and the remains of one such structure can be seen on Fort Royal Hill in Worcester, England."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Rostock_Warnem%C3%BCnde_mit_Schanze.jpg/330px-Rostock_Warnem%C3%BCnde_mit_Schanze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sconce_(fortification)","wikidata":"Q91203"},{"title":"Sconce (light fixture)","blocks":[{"text":"A sconce or wall light is a decorative light fixture that is mounted to a wall. The sconce is a very old form of fixture, historically used with candles and oil lamps."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Sconce.jpg/330px-Sconce.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sconce_(light_fixture)","wikidata":"Q6653030"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Fotothek_df_tg_0008976_Architektur_%5E_Geometrie_%5E_Festungsbau_%5E_Befestigung_%5E_Schanze.jpg/330px-Fotothek_df_tg_0008976_Architektur_%5E_Geometrie_%5E_Festungsbau_%5E_Befestigung_%5E_Schanze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Fotothek_df_tg_0008976_Architektur_%5E_Geometrie_%5E_Festungsbau_%5E_Befestigung_%5E_Schanze.jpg/330px-Fotothek_df_tg_0008976_Architektur_%5E_Geometrie_%5E_Festungsbau_%5E_Befestigung_%5E_Schanze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sconces in the shape of an enclosed redoubt; here shown as  incorporated into a verschanzten Linie or \"fortified line\". The schanze is additionally protected in this example by a couvreface.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFotothek_df_tg_0008976_Architektur_%5E_Geometrie_%5E_Festungsbau_%5E_Befestigung_%5E_Schanze.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Engelse_Schans_Lievelde.JPG/500px-Engelse_Schans_Lievelde.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Engelse_Schans_Lievelde.JPG/1280px-Engelse_Schans_Lievelde.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Engelse_Schans_Lievelde.JPG/500px-Engelse_Schans_Lievelde.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Engelse Schans (English Sconce) of 1627 in Lievelde, the Netherlands (photo 2008)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEngelse_Schans_Lievelde.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Barockschanze_Gersbach.jpg/500px-Barockschanze_Gersbach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai